] [17592186119842 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119842 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186368681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119842 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119842 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119842 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119842 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119842 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119842 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1045 "The Cartesian Equation of a Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "28cf8e1a-d07a-556d-a006-2d022a4740c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120221 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120221 #edb/ref 1069 "q-33005" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120221 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120221 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the straight line with the vector equation\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120221 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8f5992b1-761e-5564-8420-76d555a44826" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120221 #edb/ref 1074 "We have the vector equation \n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(0,-5,5)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨6,1,-6⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-0}{6}=\\frac{y-(-5)}{1}=\\frac{z-5}{-6}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{6}=\\frac{y+5}{1}=\\frac{z-5}{-6}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120243 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120243 #edb/ref 1069 "q-33234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120243 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120243 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1f/1f9599192c1979f40fc519458019ed3296d9a5ef9048aaed41aab973c62e0be8.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120243 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "deb4cbaf-5918-5a60-ba36-d2bbb72dfbda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120243 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-2,3,1)$ and $Q(5,-1,4).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨5,-1,4⟩-⟨-2,3,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7,-4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-2)}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120707 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120707 #edb/ref 1061 17885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120707 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "762cbf4d-a393-51df-98bb-da96c034848b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120707 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120708 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120708 #edb/ref 1061 17886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120708 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0e305d64-a5b0-5e37-85c9-271d9bd1785b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120708 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120709 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120709 #edb/ref 1061 17887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120709 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "15d988ae-4b19-5d9f-a7e2-fd6fd8c3f4c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120709 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120710 #edb/ref 1061 17888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120710 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1e9d9f49-2eb3-5745-9da4-70a87dddd9f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120710 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120728 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120728 #edb/ref 1061 18457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120728 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c618a49e-3866-523c-b010-33f9b474436e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120728 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120729 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120729 #edb/ref 1061 18458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120729 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "669d1115-ca36-5052-810d-cb802269d1fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120729 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121048 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we know that the vector equation of the line $l$ is given by\n![](images/92/92c5ed55727717320b9c128adf1a6d786666adb38cd6ba71d57917e60d661fd8.svg)\n\nand we want to rewrite it using Cartesian coordinates. In other words, we want to create an equation that relates $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ to each other.\n\nWe can do this using the following $3$ steps.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the parametric equations for the line.\n\nSubstituting ![](images/67/67b8360f90540ffb75d39717c375f0ef6e9a8a39cae5832c78957105e0d148b6.svg) into the equation of $l,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3+6t \\\\ -2+5t \\\\ 5-t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3+6t \\\\ y=-2+5t \\\\ z=5-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Solve for $t$ in each of the parametric equations.\n\n![](images/ee/eed0a210b121af73bc2ceab04b95def59239a742055284e706a1532b47edc60d.svg)\n\n**Step 3:** Equate the resulting expressions. Because the right-hand side of each equation is equal to $t,$ we can write this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} = t\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by omitting the \"$=t$\" on the right-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} \\,.\n$$\n\nAnd we are done! \n\nThis way of writing the equation of a line is called the **Cartesian equation** or **Cartesian canonical equation** (or simply **canonical equation**).\n\nIn general, the Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-x_0}{v_x}=\\dfrac{y-y_0}{v_y}=\\dfrac{z-z_0}{v_z} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121048 #edb/ref 1065 3245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121048 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4b2a904a-eda3-58e1-9b0f-e742454f48b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121048 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121055 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to write the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n![](images/9d/9d4a6d56170de2e0dbfa7e2a6f51905b19f9ada9fa1900aafe2672f54831be86.svg)\n\nUsing the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-3}{0} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-3}{0}.\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because $\\dfrac{z-3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/0b/0bea89e098af6be643450df08d30b97c0c3d658075b0b891ab2c4c7f37077f42.svg)\n\nIf we solve for $t$ in the parametric equations of the line, we get\n\n![](images/f3/f399dae55ae8c720f7769fa78a42b30b2a7b7aab49ed1515d9c43d371f3a9f71.svg)\n\nOnly the first two equations have right-hand sides equal to $t,$ so we combine them but keep the third equation separate:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}, \\; z=3\n$$\n\nSo, it's as if we removed the $\\dfrac{z-3}{0}$ part in the original formula and replaced it with $z=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121055 #edb/ref 1065 3316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121055 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7a2d263d-c94f-5d86-859a-c6c79c835eca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121055 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260007 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{z}{2},\\,y=5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260007 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260007 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0e3012f4-0f16-5e89-a57e-3fda5fefd0d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260169 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{x+1}{-2}=\\frac{y-5}{-1}=\\frac{z-7}{1}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260169 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260169 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "22045b16-d166-53e9-a860-057d8df291c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260306 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{x+1}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{-3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260306 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260306 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2fafacb4-7245-53f8-8b71-32255ae24867" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260636 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{z-2}{1},\\,y=5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260636 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260636 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "59250e7e-0cc1-59aa-badb-e267dbd5b96d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260705 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{x+3}{7}=\\frac{z}{2},\\,y=5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260705 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260705 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5ff911b7-cf81-5f4e-bd66-a8f69e551b29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260799 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{z-2}{2},\\,y=5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260799 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260799 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "69aca3d6-1ef4-5e05-b053-4667d30d8d92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260892 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{x+1}{8}=\\frac{y+4}{-5}=\\frac{z+5}{-3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260892 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260892 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "74476a0a-0598-5512-a162-969f54033e22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260988 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{x-1}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{5}=\\frac{z+5}{-3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260988 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260988 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8042af39-00a6-5489-835e-601f12f14e78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261083 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{x+2}{-1}=\\frac{y-5}{-1}=\\frac{z+7}{1}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186